'''Atheneum''', ''n.'' See ''Athenæum.''

'''Athenian''' (a-thē′ni-an), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< L. as if *''Athenianus'', equivalent to ''Atheniensis'', < ''Athenæ'', < Gr. Ἀθῆναι, Athens, traditionally named after Ἀθήνη, Athene.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Pertaining to Athens, anciently the metropolis of Attica in Greece, and now the capital of the kingdom of Greece.

'''II.''' ''n.'' A native or citizen of Athens.

'''atheologian†''' (ā″thē-ō-lō′ji-an), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀ- priv. (''a''-<sup>18</sup>) + ''theologian.''] One who is not a theologian; one who has no knowledge of theology; an ignorant theologian.
{{smaller block|They . . . [the Jesuits] are the only ''atheologians'' whose heads entertain no other object but the tumult of realms.
{{right|''Sir J. Hayward'', Answer to Doleman, ix.}}}}

'''atheological''' (ā″thē-ō-loj′i-kal), ''a.'' [< Gr. ἀ- priv. (''a''-<sup>18</sup>) + ''theological.''] Untheological; contrary to theology.
{{smaller block|In the curt ''atheological'' phrase of the Persian Lucretius, “one thing is certain, and the rest is lies.”
{{right|''Swinburne'', Shakespeare, p. 233.}}}}

