'''atheology†''' (ā-thē-ol′ō-ji), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀ- priv. (''a''-<sup>18</sup>) + ''theology.'' Cf. ''atheous.''] '''1.''' Lack or absence of theological knowledge; opposition to theology.—'''2.''' Atheism.
{{smaller block|Several of our learned members have written many profound treatises on anarchy, but a brief, complete body of ''atheology'' seemed yet wanting.
{{right|''Swift'', On Collins’s Discourse.}}}}

'''atheous''' (ā′thē-us), ''a.'' [= Pg. ''atheo'' = Sp. It. ''ateo'', an atheist, < L. ''atheus'', ''atheos'', < Gr. ἄθεος, without a god, godless: see ''atheism.''] '''1†.''' Atheistic; ungodly.
{{smaller block|The hypocrite or ''atheous'' priest. {{right|''Milton'', P. R., i. 487.}}}}
'''2.''' Having no reference to God; irrespective of divine existence or power.
{{smaller block|“All physical science, properly so called, is compelled by its very nature to take no account of the being of God: as soon as it does this, it trenches upon theology, and ceases to be physical science.” And so, coining a discriminating word to express this, he [the Bishop of Carlisle] would say that science was ''atheous'', and therefore could not be atheistic.
{{right|''Science'', III. 132.}}}}

'''Athericera''' (ath-ē-ris′e-rä), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < Gr. ἀθήρ, awn or beard of an ear of corn, + κέρας, a horn.] In Latreille’s system of classification, the fifth family of dipterous insects, nearly equivalent to the dichætous division of brachycerous ''Diptera'', but including the ''Syrphidæ.'' {{smaller|The division corresponded to the Linnean genera ''Conops'' and ''Œstrus'', with most of the species of ''Musca'', including the bot-flies and drone-flies with the flies proper. [Not in use.]}}

