'''athetoid''' (ath′e-toid), ''a.'' Of or resembling athetosis: as, ''athetoid'' movements.

'''athetosis''' (ath-e-tō′sis), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἄθετος, without place (see ''athetize''), + ''-osis''.] In ''pathol.'', a condition in which the hands and feet cannot be maintained in any position in which they are placed, but continually perform involuntary, slow, irregular movements.

'''athink†''', ''v. impers.'' [ME. ''athinke'', reduced form of ''ofthinke'', < AS. ''ofthyncan'', impers., < ''of-'' + ''thyncan'', seem: see ''a-''<sup>4</sup> and ''think''.] To repent; grieve.—'''Me athinketh†''', {{smaller|it repents me.}}
{{smaller block|''Me athinketh'' that I schal reherce it here.
{{right|Chaucer, Prol. to Miller’s Tale, l. 62.}}}}

'''athirst''' (a̤-thėrst′), ''a.'' [< ME. ''athurst'', also ''athreste'' and ''afurst'', contr. from ''ofthurst'', ''ofthyrst'', < AS. ''ofthyrsted'', very thirsty, pp. of ''ofthyrstan'', thirst, < ''of-'' (intensive) + ''thyrstan'', pp. ''thyrsted'', thirst: see ''a-''<sup>4</sup> and ''thirst'', ''v.''] '''1.''' Thirsty; wanting drink.
{{smaller block|When thou art ''athirst'', go unto the vessels, and drink.
{{right|Ruth ii. 9.}}}}
'''2.''' Figuratively, having a keen appetite or desire.
{{smaller block|<poem>Their bounding hearts alike
''Athirst'' for battle.</poem>
{{right|Cowper, Iliad.}}}}

